  "narrative": "The new discovery set strongly reinforces the three largest accepted dataset associations and makes them more specific. H28/R14 shows that SMARCA4 loss is still associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency when restricted to ARID1A-intact cells; the signed estimate is about +0.777 for the negative dependency-direction claim, meaning the raw exposed-minus-comparator difference is roughly -0.777. This is enormous relative to SMARCA2 score scale, so H28 is accepted. H29/R15 tests ARID1A loss among SMARCA4-intact cells and gives a tiny, direction-inconsistent contrast (signed -0.003), with a maximum hypothesis-supporting magnitude well below the approximate 10% SMARCA2 scale; H29 is rejected. Thus the SMARCA2 association looks much more specific to SMARCA4 loss than to ARID1A loss in this selected dataset.\n\nH30 and H31 accept SMARCA4 loss within adenocarcinoma and faster-growing lines, respectively. These subgroup effects are nearly as large as the global effect, although H31 is restricted to measured growth-rate cases. H32/R18 accepts the co-mutation-isolated reversed NFE2L2 finding: among STK11-intact lines, KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency (signed +0.801 for the positive direction, i.e. less negative dependency). This is very large and supports specificity to KEAP1 rather than STK11. H33/R19 tests STK11 loss among KEAP1-intact lines and is essentially null; because the NFE2L2 score distribution and registered direction make the exact 10% boundary somewhat sensitive, I leave H33 unresolved but closed as not promising. I request independent validation of H32 because it is a refined, biologically important reversal claim and is well powered.\n\nH34, H35, and H36 accept MSI-high versus WRN dependency in adenocarcinoma, faster-growing lines, and slower-growing lines, respectively. The lower bounds remain far above clinical-magnitude thresholds, even though H36 has a smaller exposed cell (25) and wider interval. The MSI-high WRN association appears robust across available subtype and growth-rate contexts. H15/R23 remains unresolved: KRAS-mutant adenocarcinoma lines show a weak positive signed contrast consistent with stronger KRAS dependency, but the interval includes both null and clinically meaningful effects; this is not enough to accept.\n\nThe control probes are reassuring but not absolutely perfect. H38/R24 tests Avana-library exposure for WRN in the registered positive direction; the signed estimate is negative and the upper bound for a positive library effect is below the approximate 10% WRN threshold, so the registered positive association is rejected. A modest inverse library association is not excluded, but it is small relative to the accepted MSI-high effect. H39/R25 tests an arbitrary research marker against SMARCA2; no clinically meaningful positive contrast is present, supporting that the SMARCA2 finding is not driven by an unassigned binary assay. Overall, the evidence now supports co-mutation-isolated and context-robust dataset associations for SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 and MSI-high/WRN, with strong dataset support for KEAP1-loss/weaker NFE2L2 that is pending independent validation.",
  "research_notes": "Current strong dataset-supported associations: (1) MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency, robust globally and in adenocarcinoma/fast/slow growth; H6 accepted, H34-H36 accepted. (2) SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H7 accepted, H28/H30/H31 accepted. H29 ARID1A-only is null, supporting SMARCA4 specificity. (3) KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H13 accepted and H32 accepted, with H33 STK11 control not supporting STK11 effect. Independent validation requested for H32.\n\nNull/control findings: Avana library positive effect on WRN rejected (H38), but possible small inverse association not excluded. Research marker E control on SMARCA2 rejected (H39). KRAS-mutation association remains weak/unresolved globally (H1) and in adenocarcinoma (H15/H23 R23). EGFR global amplified dependency was rejected after validation. Pending broad pathway claims: H14 ARID1A->SMARCA2, H16-H26 STAT3/TMED10/DHX9/POLR2A/RIT1/WRN and KRAS/EGFR interactions, H27 POLR2A library control, H37 high MYC RPL11 refinement. Next priorities: run pending pathway/interaction claims, new QC/context refinements for accepted effects, and await H32 validation. Keep conclusions limited to associations within this supplied dataset, not causal or external claims."
}

