 "narrative": "The new robustness and co-mutation analyses further strengthen the three largest dataset-supported associations while providing useful negative controls. For WRN/MSI-high, H43/R27 shows that the association remains extremely large under stricter screen-quality restriction, with signed estimate about +0.795 and lower bound +0.758, far above the approximate 10% WRN clinical-magnitude threshold around +0.03. H50/R30 shows the same near-global effect under matched RNA/DNA availability, with lower bound about +0.760. These results support accepting H43 and H50 and closing them as accepted context-robust refinements. For SMARCA2/SMARCA4 loss, H52/R31 shows the effect remains huge under matched RNA/DNA availability, lower bound about +0.760, far above the approximate SMARCA2 threshold around +0.04. H49/R29 is especially informative because it tests SMARCA4 loss specifically among ARID1A-loss lines: the signed effect is about +0.814 with lower bound +0.767, indicating the association is not weakened when SWI/SNF co-loss is present. This is a strong new accepted refinement, but because the exposed cell is small (n=76) and this is a newly accepted claim, I request independent validation of H49. For NFE2L2/KEAP1 loss, H48/R28 tests KEAP1 loss among STK11-loss lines and gives a very large positive signed effect, lower bound +0.727, far above the small NFE2L2 absolute-scale clinical threshold around +0.009. This complements the already validated STK11-intact H32 result and supports that the KEAP1 association is not limited to one co-mutational stratum.\n\nThe negative and borderline claims are mostly unhelpful. H57/R33 tests PIK3CA mutation for stronger STAT3 dependency; the signed estimate is opposite the registered direction and the upper bound for hypothesis-supporting effect is well below the approximate STAT3 threshold, so H57 is rejected. H60/R35 tests NF1 loss among KRAS-wildtype lines for stronger RIT1 dependency; the registered positive-support interval does not reach the RIT1 clinical threshold, so H60 is rejected. H61/R36 is a useful negative control using research marker D against WRN; the upper positive bound is far below the WRN threshold, supporting that arbitrary binary assay structure does not reproduce the large MSI-high effect. H56/R32 remains unresolved but closed: RB1 loss shows a small positive point estimate for WRN dependency, but the interval includes null and some clinically meaningful values, while the point estimate is not compelling. H59/R34 also remains unresolved but closed: KRAS-mutant TP53-intact lines show a small positive signed effect for KRAS dependency, but the interval includes null and values above threshold; this is not enough to accept. H16/R37 is a borderline EGFR-amplification subgroup test: it remains unresolved but closed because the point estimate is below threshold and the global EGFR claim was already independently rejected; H37/R38 similarly remains unresolved but closed because the estimate is essentially null despite an interval that barely includes clinically meaningful positive effects. Overall, the evidence continues to support WRN dependence in MSI-high lines, SMARCA2 dependence in SMARCA4-loss lines, and weaker NFE2L2 dependence in KEAP1-loss lines, with these findings remaining dataset-specific associations rather than causal or generalizable claims.",
 "research_notes": "Current core accepted dataset associations are strengthened. WRN/MSI-high is robust globally (H6/R6) and in adenocarcinoma (H34/R20), faster growth (H35/R21), slower growth (H36/R22), higher screen ROC (H43/R27), and matched RNA/DNA (H50/R30). SMARCA2/SMARCA4 loss is robust globally (H7/R7), ARID1A-intact (H28/R14 now closed accepted), adenocarcinoma (H30/R16), faster growth (H31/R17), matched RNA/DNA (H52/R31), and especially ARID1A co-loss (H49/R29, accepted and pending independent validation). NFE2L2/KEAP1 loss is accepted globally as weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H13/R8), validated among STK11-intact lines (H32/R18/R26), and also strong among STK11-loss lines (H48/R28), making it co-mutation-robust. Negative controls: marker E against SMARCA2 rejected (H39/R25), Avana positive direction against WRN rejected (H38/R24), marker D against WRN rejected (H61/R36).\n\nBorderline/non-promising claims are closed: RB1 loss for WRN (H56/R32), KRAS mutation among TP53-intact for KRAS dependency (H59/R34), EGFR adenocarcinoma subgroup (H16/R37), and high-MYC-expression RPL11 refinement (H37/R38). PIK3CA->STAT3 and NF1/KRAS-WT->RIT1 are rejected for registered directions. KRAS mutation claims H1/H15 remain unresolved but lower priority. Next analyses should prioritize remaining robustness tests for accepted effects: screen ROC >=0.97 (H44), Avana-library subsets for WRN/SMARCA2/NFE2L2 (H45-H47), low-serum restrictions (H51,H53), and broader untested pathway claims H14,H17-H24,H25-H27,H40-H42,H58. Interpretations remain limited to associations in this supplied dataset.",
