  "narrative": "The new robustness analyses strongly support the three major dataset-specific associations and do not materially change their interpretation. Approximate 10% relative thresholds based on population absolute means are about +0.030 for WRN, +0.042 for SMARCA2, +0.009 for NFE2L2, +0.025 for EGFR, +0.026 for ERBB2, +0.026 for RIT1, and +0.018 for USP7. For WRN/MSI-high, H69/R41 in high-mutation-burden lines gives a signed effect +0.782 with lower bound +0.749, and H70/R42 in the alternative KY library gives +0.732 with lower bound +0.675. Both intervals are far above the WRN clinical-magnitude threshold and far from null. H69 therefore addresses the concern that MSI-high is merely a generic high-TMB marker: the WRN association persists among lines selected for relatively high mutation burden. H70 addresses platform specificity: the association is still enormous in KY, despite the exposed cell having only 32 lines. These accept H69 and H70 as robust dataset-specific refinements, though I note the small exposed cell in H70 and rely on the consistency with the global effect and many prior strata. For SMARCA2/SMARCA4 loss, H71/R43 under very high screen-quality restriction and H72/R44 in KY both yield huge positive signed effects (+0.793 and +0.816) with lower bounds above +0.75. These are well above the approximate SMARCA2 threshold and support accepting both as robust dataset-specific refinements. For NFE2L2/KEAP1 loss, H73/R45 under very high screen-quality restriction and H74/R46 in KY both yield very large weaker-dependency effects (+0.797 and +0.794) with lower bounds above +0.75. These are far above the small NFE2L2 absolute-mean threshold and support accepting both. Together, H69-H74 strengthen the core triad against mutation-burden, screen-quality, and library-platform concerns. The interaction results are much less informative. H64/R47 asks whether the SMARCA4-loss effect on SMARCA2 differs by ARID1A loss; the interaction is +0.037 with interval -0.015 to +0.089. This is near the SMARCA2 threshold but includes null and cannot establish meaningful effect modification. Given the already accepted within-stratum effects in ARID1A-intact and ARID1A-loss groups, I leave it unresolved and closed. H66/R48 asks whether the KEAP1-loss effect on NFE2L2 differs by STK11 loss; the interaction is -0.026 with interval -0.079 to +0.026. It includes null and supports neither a clinically meaningful enhancement nor attenuation in the registered direction, so it remains unresolved and closed. RTK isolation tests are mostly null. H75/R49 tests stronger ERBB2 dependency among EGFR-amplification-negative lines and gives a near-zero estimate +0.001 with interval -0.028 to +0.030. Because the interval includes null and clinically meaningful positive values, H75 is unresolved but closed. H76/R50 tests weaker EGFR dependency among ERBB2-amplification-negative lines and gives signed estimate -0.027 with interval -0.053 to -0.001. For the registered weaker-dependency direction, this interval is entirely opposite the hypothesis, so H76 is rejected. It does suggest the opposite possibility that EGFR amplification may be associated with stronger EGFR dependency when ERBB2 amplification is absent, but the opposite-direction interval after sign reversal would only barely clear null and not robustly clear threshold, so I registered a new unresolved refinement rather than accepting it. H79/R51 tests BRAF V600E stronger RIT1 dependency among KRAS-wildtype lines; the signed point estimate is negative and the upper bound +0.021 is below the approximate RIT1 threshold +0.026. The interval does not support a clinically meaningful RIT1-dependency increase, so H79 is rejected and closed. H82/R52 tests MYC amplification stronger USP7 dependency among high MYC-expression lines. The signed point estimate +0.029 is above the approximate USP7 threshold +0.018, but the interval -0.037 to +0.095 is wide, includes null, and includes clinically meaningful opposite values. There is not enough evidence to accept or reject clinically meaningful USP7 association, so H82 remains unresolved and closed. I request independent validation of H6, the original global WRN/MSI-high association, because WRN is the largest accepted dataset-specific association and, unlike SMARCA2 and NFE2L2, it has not yet received independent validation. If validation remains large and positive, it will substantially strengthen confidence that the WRN/MSI-high finding is not a discovery-set artifact. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset, not causal claims or generalizable biology.",
  "research_notes": "Core accepted dataset-specific triad remains strong and now more robust: (1) MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency: global H6/R6, robust H34-H36, H43/R27, H50/R30, H69/R41 high TMB, H70/R42 KY library. Validation of original H6 requested to confirm independent evidence. (2) SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency: global H7/R7, validated H28/R14+R40 and H49/R29+R39, robust H30/R16, H31/R17, H52/R31, H71/R43 high screen quality, H72/R44 KY library. (3) KEAP1 loss -> much weaker NFE2L2 dependency: global reversed H13/R8, validated H32/R18+R26, robust H48/R28 STK11-loss, H73/R45 high screen quality, H74/R46 KY library. Interactions near null: H64 SMARCA4 x ARID1A unresolved closed; H66 KEAP1 x STK11 unresolved closed. RTK isolation: H75 ERBB2 amp among EGFR-negative unresolved closed; H76 EGFR amp among ERBB2-negative in weaker direction rejected, but opposite stronger direction registered as new unresolved parent H76. Other rejected/unresolved: H79 BRAF among KRAS-WT stronger RIT1 rejected because upper bound below RIT1 threshold; H82 MYC amp among high MYC-expression stronger USP7 unresolved closed due wide interval. Future priorities: assess independent H6 validation at synthesis; remaining untested high-MYC-expression claims H80 DHX9, H81 POLR2A, H83 KIF18A, H84 STAT3; possibly KRAS/MDR2/RTK co-alteration or quality-restricted tests only if they add meaningful specificity. All interpretations remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims.",
