  "narrative": "I propose effect-modification and specificity tests that follow from the strongest dataset-specific findings rather than repeating mean-difference presence tests that have already been shown or rejected. First, PTEN-loss/STAT3 is now accepted and independently validated, but its internal interpretation still needs to be distinguished from a general co-alteration effect or a broader PI3K-TP53-RAS network effect. Interactions of PTEN loss with PIK3CA mutation, TP53 loss, and KRAS mutation test whether the PTEN effect is amplified, attenuated, or essentially constant across these common co-drivers. The additional PTEN analysis restricted to TP53-intact and PIK3CA-intact lines asks whether the global PTEN/STAT3 association survives when two likely co-mutant contexts are removed. A clinically meaningful attenuated effect in these strata would suggest that the global estimate is context-dependent or confounded by co-alteration patterns, whereas persistence would strengthen the association in this dataset. Second, I add an interaction for the unresolved MDM2/TP53-loss comparison with CDKN2A loss. The global MDM2 estimate is small, but effect modification could be clinically relevant even when the mean difference is not, and the TP53-CDKN2A p53-axis context is scientifically motivated. Third, the core WRN/MSI-high, SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss, and NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss associations are already robust in mean-difference tests within KY library and adenocarcinoma subsets. The new interaction tests ask whether the size of those associations changes by library or subtype. A near-null interaction would argue that the large effects are broadly shared across these technical and histologic contexts; a large positive or negative interaction would identify important subgroup-specific attenuation or amplification. Fourth, PTEN loss is tested against KRAS dependency as a broad specificity probe. PTEN loss could plausibly alter KRAS or STAT3 through PI3K/RAS feedback pathways, so a large PTEN/KRAS association would reduce the outcome specificity of PTEN/STAT3. A near-null PTEN/KRAS result, together with specificity tests already registered for SMARCA2 and WRN, would make PTEN/STAT3 appear more selective in this dataset. All proposals remain comparisons of measured associations within the supplied lung cell-line dataset and should not be interpreted as causal gene dependency or generalizable biology until independently replicated or externally contextualized.",
  "research_notes": "Updated accepted dataset-specific associations: WRN/MSI-high is dramatically stronger WRN dependency, globally validated and robust to TMB, Cas9, screen quality, KY library, growth, lineage, and matched omics. SMARCA4-loss is dramatically stronger SMARCA2 dependency, globally accepted and independently validated in ARID1A-intact H28/R40 and ARID1A-loss H49/R39, robust to Cas9, TMB, screen quality, KY library, growth, and matched omics. KEAP1-loss is dramatically weaker NFE2L2 dependency, globally accepted via H13, validated in STK11-intact H32/R26, and robust across STK11-loss, high screen quality, KY library, Cas9, fast growth, and matched omics. PTEN-loss is substantially stronger STAT3 dependency, accepted and independently validated globally H93/R61+R66. Next active priorities: test PTEN/STAT3 effect modification by PIK3CA mutation, TP53 loss, and KRAS mutation; test PTEN/STAT3 in TP53- and PIK3CA-intact lines; test PTEN specificity versus KRAS dependency. Test interaction robustness of the core WRN/SMARCA2/NFE2L2 associations by KY library and adenocarcinoma subtype. Test MDM2/TP53 interaction with CDKN2A loss because the global effect is small but interaction could be biologically meaningful. Remaining active controls and context tests include high-MYC-expression dependencies, EGFR reversal claims only if new interval evidence appears, research markers, low-serum robustness, and ARID1A/SMARCA4 context tests. Interpret all results as supplied-lung-cell-line associations, not causal dependencies or generalizable biology."
}

