  "narrative": "Final synthesis report.\n\nDue assessments.\n\nH146 (CDKN2A loss versus CDKN2A-intact on dependency_MDM2 globally): discovery R92 estimated +0.117 with 95% Welch interval +0.082 to +0.152, well above the approximate 10-percent clinical-significance threshold for MDM2 of about +0.027. The independent validation result R93 estimated +0.114 with stringent interval +0.067 to +0.161, cells 435 versus 1565. This validates both the direction and the clinically meaningful magnitude in the supplied dataset. I accept H146 and close the global CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 investigation.\n\nH183 (CDKN2A loss versus CDKN2A-intact on dependency_MDM2 within TP53-loss, MSI-intact, KEAP1-intact, PTEN-intact lines): discovery R109 estimated +0.118 with interval +0.022 to +0.214, excluding null but falling slightly below the approximate +0.027 clinical threshold at the lower bound. The new validation result R119 estimated a larger point difference, +0.165, but its stringent interval was -0.011 to +0.340, cells 46 versus 203. Thus the validation is direction-consistent but very imprecise; it neither confirms a clinically meaningful CDKN2A effect in this clean TP53-loss context nor refutes it. I downgrade H183 to unresolved and keep the investigation active. This is not a rejection of the global CDKN2A/MDM2 axis, which is strongly supported by H146; it indicates that the TP53-loss-specific clean context remains too uncertain for a clinically meaningful claim.\n\nSupported findings.\n\nThe supplied lung cell-line dataset supports five major marker-dependency association axes, all as associations rather than causal dependencies: MSI-high is associated with far stronger WRN dependency; SMARCA4 loss is associated with far stronger SMARCA2 dependency; KEAP1 loss is associated with far weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss is associated with substantially stronger STAT3 dependency; and CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. The CDKN2A/MDM2 global association is now independently validated with a lower bound well above the clinical threshold. Several CDKN2A/MDM2 context refinements are accepted: high screen-quality contexts, high Cas9-activity contexts, TP53-loss high-screen-quality lines, high MYC expression, KY library, slow growth, KRAS-intact lines, and clean TP53/PTEN/MSI-intact lines. These support robustness of the global CDKN2A/MDM2 association across many measured contexts.\n\nNegative or non-supported findings.\n\nBroad driver-gene dependency hypotheses remain unsupported. BRAF V600E, EGFR amplification, and ERBB2 amplification were not accepted as clinically meaningful markers of stronger BRAF, EGFR, or ERBB2 dependency. KRAS mutation, NF1 loss, STK11 loss, and PTEN loss were not accepted as markers of clinically meaningful stronger KRAS dependency. MYC-amplified lines with high MYC expression did not show clinically meaningful stronger RPL11 dependency, and validation rejected the claimed direction and magnitude. Specificity probes for CDKN2A loss rejected clinically meaningful cross-marker effects on WRN in MSI-intact lines, STAT3 in PTEN-intact lines, and SMARCA2 in SMARCA4-intact lines. Several interaction analyses did not support lineage subtype, TP53 status, PIK3CA status, KRAS status, or library status as decisive effect modifiers, although some remain unresolved because intervals are wide rather than decisively null.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty.\n\nThe principal remaining uncertainty is the magnitude of CDKN2A loss on MDM2 dependency specifically within TP53-loss lines. H161 remains accepted as an association based on discovery and validation excluding null, but its validation lower bound is near zero, and the clean TP53-loss context H183 is now unresolved after wide validation uncertainty. A second unresolved question is whether CDKN2A loss is specific to MDM2 relative to USP7: global USP7 and TP53-loss/USP7 specificity probes remain inconclusive, although other cross-marker specificity probes were reassuringly negative. The borderline EGFR signal in ERBB2-intact, TP53-intact lines remains unresolved and is not accepted as robust. Assay-quality and lineage modifiers are mostly unsupported, but not all possibilities are fully excluded when intervals include clinically meaningful values.\n\nMost informative next analyses.\n\nI registered four follow-up comparisons. First, a stricter high-screen-quality TP53-loss MDM2 test asks whether the H183 uncertainty is due to low-quality or small-screen noise in the clean context. Second, a Cas9-quality TP53-loss MDM2 test asks whether stronger editing quality improves the evidence for clinically meaningful magnitude. Third, a Cas9-quality TP53-loss USP7 test probes CDKN2A specificity while reducing assay noise. Fourth, a high-screen-quality interaction test asks whether TP53 loss modifies the CDKN2A-MDM2 association after limiting to more reliable screens. These analyses are expected to be more informative than additional broad driver-gene screening or further validation of already rejected axes. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations and do not establish causal CRISPR dependency relationships or external generalizability.",
