  "narrative": "The main new finding is H187: TP53 loss versus intact is associated with a large signed positive mean difference in dependency_USP7 (R123 +0.396; 95% Welch interval +0.325 to +0.467; cells 406/1594). With direction +1, this means TP53-loss lines have higher, i.e. less negative, USP7 dependency scores than TP53-intact lines: weaker USP7 dependency. The interval is far above the approximate 10% absolute-mean clinical threshold for USP7 (about +0.018), so I accept this as a dataset-specific association and request independent validation. I treat it as hypothesis-supporting evidence for a p53/USP7 axis, not proof of mechanism or external generalizability. Co-alteration confounding is plausible because TP53 loss is common and may correlate with other accepted markers, so I registered context and specificity tests: high-screen-quality TP53-loss, high-Cas9-activity TP53-loss, CDKN2A-intact/high-screen-quality TP53-loss, and a TP53-by-CDKN2A interaction on USP7. The CDKN2A-USP7 probes H170, H185, and H196 remain unresolved because intervals are wide and span clinically meaningful positive and negative effects; they are deferred pending the TP53-specific follow-up. H186, the CDKN2A-by-TP53 interaction on MDM2 in higher-quality screens, is unresolved and deferred: it is conceptually important but too imprecise to accept or reject a clinically meaningful interaction. The MYC-amplification/fast-proliferation probes for POLR2A, KIF18A, and DHX9 did not support clinically meaningful stronger dependency, and the intervals remain too wide for a clean null claim; because these were lower-prior broad hypotheses, I closed them without rejection. The TMED10 EGFR-amplification adenocarcinoma test and the EGFR-amplification RIT1 test in KRAS-intact lines are also unresolved closed because they are low-prior and not currently worth further allocation. The TP53 interaction tests for SMARCA4-SMARCA2 and KEAP1-NFE2L2 are deferred because the underlying axes are accepted and modification by TP53 status could matter, but the new intervals are imprecise and do not establish clinically meaningful interactions.",
